      "parts": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "\"When using the engine's StandardMaterial on the WebGPU backend, several rendering features were producing incorrect results or causing shader compilation failures. The problem manifested as missing or distorted textures, wrong normal perturbations, broken depth‑pre‑pass output, and visual artifacts when applying decals, morph‑target tangents, or refraction effects. The underlying issue was that the shader code referenced undefined or stale variables for UV coordinates, applied uniform scaling incorrectly, and returned the wrong type of output for the depth‑pre‑pass pass. The required behavior is that all of these features work seamlessly on WebGPU: \n\n- Detail maps must sample using the fragment's UV coordinates and any UV offset, and the detail normal must be scaled using the material's detail‑info uniform before being blended with the base normal. \n- The depth‑pre‑pass pass must write a solid black color to the appropriate output structure and then return that structure, ensuring the pass completes without errors. \n- Morph‑target processing must correctly update tangent data when tangent morph targets are enabled, using the per‑vertex morph influence values. \n- Decal sampling must also use the fragment's decal UV coordinates, respecting any UV offset, so that decals appear in the right location and orientation. \n- Refraction calculations must build the refraction UV vector using the scene view matrix and the material's refraction matrix, producing correct texture coordinates for both 2‑D and cube‑map refraction. \n- All of the above changes must preserve existing functionality for other features and must not introduce new shader compilation warnings. The engine should render scenes that combine any subset of these features without visual glitches or crashes on WebGPU.\"\n",
          "id": "prt_ed6024d39001aG8w8BlVFjoIuB",
          "sessionID": "ses_129fdb2e1ffeDQCNf7C7B3iPxW",
          "messageID": "msg_ed6024d380014jV3XTP9g2MRLa"
        }
      ]
